# Every file with a shebang must be LF in the working tree, not just in the index.
#
# Git normalises to LF in the index via core.autocrlf, but on Windows the working
# TREE keeps CRLF — so copying one of these out of a checkout onto Linux without
# git ships a file the kernel refuses to execute. `#!/usr/bin/env node\r` fails as
# `env: 'node\r': No such file or directory`.
#
# The original rule here was `*.sh` alone, which missed the file with the most to
# lose: install.sh chmod +x's bin/wildcard-perms and registers its BARE PATH as
# the PostToolUse hook command, so a CRLF copy fails on every single tool call.
# Measured 2026-09-10, working tree vs index (all four had 0 CR in the index):
# bin/wildcard-perms 874 CR bytes, memory/recall.py 529, memory/bench/bench_embed.py
# 446, scripts/package.mjs 55.
#
# Deliberately NOT `* text=auto`: the FIVE tracked .ps1 files (install.ps1,
# uninstall.ps1, memory/bench/run.ps1, scripts/verify-release.ps1,
# scripts/verify-installers.ps1) have no shebang, are Windows-only, and CRLF is
# correct for them. That list said "four" and omitted verify-installers.ps1 until
# 2026-09-15; a hand-maintained count beside the thing it counts drifts.
#
# Adding rules here does not renormalise an existing checkout — that needs
# `git add --renormalize .` once, which is why the earlier claim that "the working
# tree matches the index" was true only for a fresh clone.

*.sh  text eol=lf
*.py  text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf

# Extensionless, so no glob reaches it. This is the hook itself.
bin/wildcard-perms text eol=lf

# The one shebang'd .js. NOT a blanket `*.js`: every other tracked .js is CRLF in the
# working tree here, so a blanket rule would renormalise the whole tree for no gain.
# Measured 2026-09-15: 705 CR in the working tree against 0 in the index, which is
# exactly the drift this file's header describes, regressed for a file added after
# that fix landed. Nothing guards this; no test mentions .gitattributes.
scripts/check-line-refs.js text eol=lf
